The two methods in `BeanPostProcessor` run at different points in the bean lifecycle, and the distinction determines which features each hook can implement.

**`postProcessBeforeInitialization(Object bean, String beanName)`** runs after dependency injection but before `@PostConstruct`. This is where Spring processes annotations that configure the bean — `@Autowired`, `@Value`, `@Resource`. The bean receives its dependencies in this phase.

**`postProcessAfterInitialization(Object bean, String beanName)`** runs after `@PostConstruct` and all initialization callbacks. This is where Spring creates **proxies**. The method can return a different object than the one it received — and that replacement becomes the bean that other components inject. `@Transactional`, `@Async`, `@Cacheable`, and AOP aspects all create proxies in this hook.

```java
@Component
public class TimingBeanPostProcessor implements BeanPostProcessor {
    @Override
    public Object postProcessBeforeInitialization(Object bean, String beanName) {
        // Runs before @PostConstruct
        return bean;  // Return the same bean, or a modified/replaced one
    }

    @Override
    public Object postProcessAfterInitialization(Object bean, String beanName) {
        // Runs after @PostConstruct — can wrap in proxy
        if (bean.getClass().isAnnotationPresent(Timed.class)) {
            return createTimingProxy(bean);
        }
        return bean;
    }
}
```

Returning a different object from `postProcessAfterInitialization()` is how proxy-based features work. The original `OrderService` instance is created, initialized, then replaced by a proxy that adds transactional behavior around each method call. Every other bean that injects `OrderService` gets the proxy, not the original.

The most practical way to understand `BeanPostProcessor` is to build one. The following example creates a **custom `@Timed` annotation** that automatically logs how long each method takes — similar to what Micrometer or Spring AOP would do, but implemented from scratch to show the mechanism.

The processor scans every bean for methods annotated with `@Timed`. When it finds one, it wraps the **original bean in a CGLIB proxy** that intercepts method calls and measures execution time. This is the same pattern Spring uses internally for `@Transactional` and `@Cacheable`.

```java
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target(ElementType.METHOD)
public @interface Timed {}

@Component
public class TimingPostProcessor implements BeanPostProcessor {
    @Override
    public Object postProcessAfterInitialization(Object bean, String name) {
        boolean hasTimed = Arrays.stream(bean.getClass().getMethods())
            .anyMatch(m -> m.isAnnotationPresent(Timed.class));
        if (!hasTimed) return bean;

        // Return a proxy that wraps the original bean
        return Enhancer.create(bean.getClass(), (MethodInterceptor) (obj, method, args, proxy) -> {
            if (!method.isAnnotationPresent(Timed.class))
                return proxy.invokeSuper(obj, args);
            long start = System.nanoTime();
            Object result = proxy.invokeSuper(obj, args);
            log.info("{}.{} took {}ms", name, method.getName(),
                (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000);
            return result;
        });
    }
}
```

The key insight is the **return value**. The `postProcessAfterInitialization` method returns either the original bean (no processing needed) or a new object (the proxy). Spring replaces the bean in the container with whatever this method returns. This is why `@Transactional` works — the bean registered in the container is a proxy, not the original class.

This pattern has a practical limitation worth noting. Because the proxy is created during `postProcessAfterInitialization`, it happens once at startup for singleton beans. The performance overhead of the proxy is negligible for most applications — CGLIB proxies add roughly 1-2 microseconds per method call. But if you're writing a post-processor that wraps thousands of beans, the startup time impact can be measurable. Profile before optimizing.

Writing a custom `Condition` is straightforward — implement the interface, check whatever you need from the `ConditionContext`, and return a boolean. The context gives you access to the **`Environment`** (properties), the **`BeanDefinitionRegistry`** (existing beans), the **`ClassLoader`** (classpath checks), and the **`ResourceLoader`** (file existence).

A practical use case is **feature flags**. Instead of cluttering your code with `if (featureEnabled)` checks, you conditionally register the entire bean graph for a feature based on a property. The condition evaluates at bean definition time — before any bean is instantiated — so disabled features have zero runtime cost.

```java
public class FeatureEnabledCondition implements Condition {
    @Override
    public boolean matches(ConditionContext context,
                           AnnotatedTypeMetadata metadata) {
        // Read the feature name from the annotation attributes
        Map<String, Object> attrs = metadata
            .getAnnotationAttributes(ConditionalOnFeature.class.getName());
        String feature = (String) attrs.get("value");
        return "true".equals(
            context.getEnvironment().getProperty("feature." + feature + ".enabled"));
    }
}

@Target({ElementType.TYPE, ElementType.METHOD})
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Conditional(FeatureEnabledCondition.class)
public @interface ConditionalOnFeature {
    String value();
}

// Usage — bean only exists when feature.dark-mode.enabled=true
@Configuration
@ConditionalOnFeature("dark-mode")
public class DarkModeConfig {
    @Bean public ThemeService themeService() { return new DarkThemeService(); }
}
```

When `feature.dark-mode.enabled` is absent or `false`, the entire `DarkModeConfig` class is skipped — no `ThemeService` bean exists in the context. This is cleaner than wrapping business logic in conditionals because the bean graph itself reflects the application's active features.

The `ConditionContext` is the key to writing powerful conditions. Beyond property checks, you can inspect the classpath with `context.getClassLoader()`, check existing bean definitions with `context.getRegistry()`, and read resource files with `context.getResourceLoader()`. Spring Boot's built-in conditions use all of these — `@ConditionalOnClass` checks the classloader, `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` checks the registry.

While SpEL supports a rich expression language, production codebases typically use only a few patterns. The three most common are **bean references**, **property transformations**, and **conditional defaults**.

```java
// 1. Reference another bean's property
@Value("#{configService.getMaxRetries()}")
private int maxRetries;

// 2. Transform a property — split CSV into a List
@Value("#{'${allowed.origins}'.split(',')}")
private List<String> allowedOrigins;

// 3. Conditional default — use environment-aware logic
@Value("#{systemProperties['os.name'].contains('Windows') ? 'C:/tmp' : '/tmp'}")
private String tempDir;
```

Notice the nesting in pattern 2: `${}` resolves the property first, then `#{}` applies SpEL to the result. The order matters — `${}` is always inside `#{}`, never the reverse. This combined syntax is the standard pattern for converting comma-separated properties into Java collections.

SpEL can also access **static methods and constants**: `#{T(java.lang.Math).PI}` returns 3.14159, and `#{T(java.util.UUID).randomUUID().toString()}` generates a UUID. The `T()` operator provides access to any class on the classpath.
